# Tutorial 08 — Character Movement and Easing
# How to move characters across the screen smoothly with the [move] command and easing curves.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_08_movement
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender left happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: So far, characters have been teleporting — [show] places them somewhere instantly.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: But what if you want a character to {i}walk{/i} across the screen? That's where {b}[move]{/b} comes in.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: What would you like to explore?
> The [move] command -> section_move
> Easing curves -> section_easing
> Custom positions with "at" -> section_custom_pos
> Combining movement with dialogue -> section_move_dialogue
> Quick reference card -> reference_sheet
> Practice scene -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── The Move Command ────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_move
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: {b}[move]{/b} slides a character from their current position to a new one over time.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[move lavender center easeInOut 600]{/color}
Lavender: That moves me to center using an easeInOut curve over 600 milliseconds.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The structure is: {b}[move id position easing duration]{/b}
narrator: {b}id{/b} — the character to move.
narrator: {b}position{/b} — where to move them (left, center, right, far_left, far_right, or at X,Y).
narrator: {b}easing{/b} — the acceleration curve (how the speed changes during the move).
narrator: {b}duration{/b} — how long the move takes in milliseconds.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Unlike [show], which is instant, [move] takes time. The player watches the character glide across the screen.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: It adds life to scenes — characters feel like they exist in physical space instead of popping in and out.
> Next: Easing -> section_easing
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Easing Curves ───────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_easing
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Easing controls {i}how{/i} the movement accelerates and decelerates.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The most common options:
narrator: {b}linear{/b} — constant speed, start to finish. Mechanical, robotic.
narrator: {b}easeIn{/b} — starts slow, accelerates. Like a car pulling away from a stop.
narrator: {b}easeOut{/b} — starts fast, decelerates. Like a car braking to a stop.
narrator: {b}easeInOut{/b} — slow start, fast middle, slow stop. The most natural-looking option.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: For most character movement, {b}easeInOut{/b} is your go-to. It looks natural and smooth.
Lavender: Use {b}linear{/b} when something should feel mechanical — a sliding door, a conveyor belt.
Lavender: Use {b}easeIn{/b} when something is leaving — it builds momentum as it goes.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: The easing makes a huge difference in feel. Try changing it on the same move and watch how it changes the character's "personality."
> Next: Custom positions -> section_custom_pos
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Custom Positions ────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_custom_pos
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Named positions (left, center, right) are convenient, but sometimes you need exact placement.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[move lavender at 0.30,0.80 easeInOut 500]{/color}
Lavender: That moves me to 30% from the left and 80% from the top — precise coordinates on a 0-to-1 scale.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: You can also define reusable custom positions:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@position doorway 0.85 0.82{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[move lavender doorway easeOut 400]{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Named custom positions make your scripts more readable — "doorway" tells you more than "0.85,0.82".
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Use named positions for spots characters visit often. Use inline "at X,Y" for one-off placements.
> Next: Movement with dialogue -> section_move_dialogue
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Movement with Dialogue ──────────────────────────────────────

@label section_move_dialogue
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Movement and dialogue work great together. The key is timing.
Lavender: You can move a character {i}before{/i} they speak:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[move lavender center easeInOut 400]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[wait 400]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Okay, I'm here. What did you need?{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Notice the {b}[wait 400]{/b} — it holds until the move finishes, so the dialogue appears after she arrives.
Lavender: Without the wait, the dialogue would appear while she's still sliding, which can feel rushed.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: You can also have a character speak {i}while{/i} moving — just skip the wait:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[move lavender right easeInOut 800]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: I'll be right over there if you need me.{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Characters talking while walking is surprisingly effective. It creates energy and makes the scene feel dynamic.
> Reference card -> reference_sheet
> Try it yourself -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Reference Card ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label reference_sheet
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Movement cheat sheet:
narrator: {b}[move id position easing duration]{/b} — smoothly move a character.
narrator: {b}Positions:{/b} far_left, left, center, right, far_right, or {b}at X,Y{/b}.
narrator: {b}Easing:{/b} linear, easeIn, easeOut, easeInOut.
narrator: {b}Duration:{/b} milliseconds (300–800 is typical).
narrator: {b}@position name X Y{/b} — define a reusable custom position.
narrator: {b}[wait N]{/b} after [move] — hold until the move finishes.
narrator: {b}Tip:{/b} easeInOut is the default choice for natural-looking movement.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Movement adds a lot of polish for very little effort. Even one or two [move]s per scene makes a big difference.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice Scene ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Let's see movement in context. Pick a scene:
> A character approaches -> practice_approach
> A character retreats -> practice_retreat
> A dramatic entrance -> practice_entrance

@label practice_approach
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: Hey, come closer for a second...
[move lavender center easeInOut 600]
[wait 600]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: There. That felt natural, right? EaseInOut over 600ms — smooth and deliberate.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The slow start and stop make it feel like I actually walked there, not like I was pushed on a track.
[set practice_result 1]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_retreat
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: I... I need some space.
[move lavender far_right easeIn 500]
[wait 500]
[show lavender far_right talking]
Lavender: EaseIn — I started slow and sped up. It reads as pulling away, reluctant at first then committed.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Movement direction and easing together tell the emotional story.
[set practice_result 2]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_entrance
[hide lavender]
[wait 400]
narrator: The room was quiet. Then — footsteps, getting closer.
[show lavender far_right talking]
[move lavender center easeOut 700]
[wait 700]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Surprise! EaseOut makes the entrance feel fast and decisive — I burst in and slowed to a stop.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: For dramatic entrances, pair [hide], a narrator beat, then [show] + [move] together.
[set practice_result 3]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_wrap
[show lavender center happy]
[if practice_result == 1]
Lavender: Approaching movement is warm and inviting. It draws the character toward the player.
[elif practice_result == 2]
Lavender: Retreating movement creates emotional distance. The easing choice says a lot about {i}why{/i} they're leaving.
[else]
Lavender: Entrances set the tone for everything that follows. A fast entrance says confidence; a slow one says caution.
[endif]
Lavender: Try swapping the easing types in this file. Same move, completely different feel.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_2]
